# Vertical Order Traversal of a Binary Tree

**Problem:** [LeetCode 987 – Vertical Order Traversal of a Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/vertical-order-traversal-of-a-binary-tree/)

## Approach: DFS + Ordered Map + Multiset

### Theory

We use Depth-First Search (DFS) to traverse the binary tree and assign each node a coordinate `(row, col)`.

- Moving left decreases the column by 1 and increases the row by 1.
- Moving right increases the column by 1 and increases the row by 1.
- An ordered map stores nodes according to their column and row.
- A multiset stores node values at the same coordinate in ascending order, handling cases where multiple nodes occupy the same position.

Finally, traverse the map in ascending column order, then ascending row order, and append the sorted values to the result.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
    map<int, map<int, multiset<int>>> nodes;

    void dfs(TreeNode* root, int col, int row) {
        if (!root) return;

        nodes[col][row].insert(root->val);

        dfs(root->left, col - 1, row + 1);
        dfs(root->right, col + 1, row + 1);
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int>> verticalTraversal(TreeNode* root) {
        dfs(root, 0, 0);

        vector<vector<int>> ans;

        for (auto& col : nodes) {
            vector<int> temp;

            for (auto& row : col.second) {
                temp.insert(temp.end(),
                            row.second.begin(),
                            row.second.end());
            }

            ans.push_back(temp);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let \(n\) be the number of nodes in the binary tree.

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n\log n)\) — Each node is inserted into an ordered map and multiset, with logarithmic insertion cost. Building the final result takes \(O(n)\) overall.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(n)\) — The maps and multisets store all nodes, and the DFS recursion stack requires up to \(O(h)\) space, where \(h\) is the tree height.

## Key Learnings

- Using nested ordered maps to organize data by multiple coordinates.
- Using a multiset to sort values at identical positions.
- Applying DFS to track row and column positions.
- Understanding the ordering rules for vertical traversal in a binary tree.
